---

# Purpose

The Navigation System defines how users move throughout Aura.

Navigation should become invisible.

Users should never stop to think where something is located.

Navigation should always feel predictable.

---

# Navigation Principles

Aura follows Apple's Human Interface Guidelines while introducing a simplified navigation model centered around experiences instead of devices.

The user should always know:

• Where they are
• What is happening
• How to go back
• What the next action is

---

# Primary Navigation

Aura uses a fixed bottom navigation bar.

Tabs

Home

Rooms

Scenes

Devices

Settings

Maximum:

Five tabs.

Never more.

---

# Home

The starting point.

Opening Aura always returns the user here unless continuing an unfinished task.

Purpose

Current home status

Suggestions

Quick Actions

Favorite devices

Current room

Current scene

---

# Navigation Depth

Maximum depth:

Level 1

Tab

↓

Level 2

Detail

↓

Level 3

Configuration

Never deeper.

If a fourth level becomes necessary, redesign the feature.

---

# Navigation Style

Use NavigationStack.

Never mix multiple navigation systems.

Avoid nested NavigationStacks.

---

# Screen Presentation

Push Navigation

Used for:

Room Details

Device Details

Scene Details

Automation Details

---

Modal Sheets

Used for:

Quick Settings

Scene Picker

Device Picker

Filters

Short forms

Small configuration tasks

Preferred heights:

Medium

Large

---

Full Screen Covers

Reserved only for:

Onboarding

First-time setup

Critical pairing flows

Authentication

Emergency recovery

Never use full screen unnecessarily.

---

# Back Navigation

Users should always be able to return with:

Navigation Back

Swipe Gesture

System Back

No custom back buttons.

---

# Context Menus

Context menus expose advanced functionality.

Never hide primary actions.

Examples

Rename

Favorite

Diagnostics

Remove

Information

---

# Long Press

Long press is optional.

Never required.

Examples

Device card

↓

Long Press

↓

Rename

Diagnostics

Restart

Move

Favorite

---

# Swipe Actions

Allowed

Favorite

Delete

Archive

Disable

Not allowed

Complex workflows

Confirmation dialogs

Multi-step actions

---

# Search

Universal Search is available from:

Home

Rooms

Devices

Scenes

Settings

Search is always one tap away.

---

# Deep Linking

Aura supports direct navigation into:

Room

Device

Scene

Automation

Settings

Diagnostics

Every screen must be uniquely addressable.

---

# Universal Links

Future versions may support

aura://room/living-room

aura://scene/movie-night

aura://device/tv

These links should restore navigation state automatically.

---

# State Restoration

Aura remembers:

Last selected room

Expanded cards

Scroll position

Selected scene

Current navigation path

Users should continue exactly where they left off.

---

# Tab Switching

Switching tabs should never reset state.

Each tab preserves:

Navigation

Scroll position

Selected filters

Expanded cards

---

# Multiwindow

Future iPad support

Each window maintains its own navigation state.

No shared navigation history.

---

# Error Navigation

Errors should provide direct navigation to the relevant screen.

Example

TV Offline

↓

Open TV Diagnostics

↓

Retry Connection

Never force the user to search manually.

---

# Pairing Flow

New Device

↓

Discovery

↓

Selection

↓

Authentication

↓

Confirmation

↓

Device Detail

Users should always understand where they are in the process.

---

# Scene Creation Flow

Scenes should follow a wizard.

Name

↓

Choose Devices

↓

Choose Actions

↓

Preview

↓

Save

Users may return to previous steps at any time.

---

# Automation Creation Flow

Trigger

↓

Conditions

↓

Actions

↓

Preview

↓

Save

---

# Navigation Animations

Push

Slide

Sheet

Spring

Dismiss

Fade

Animations should communicate hierarchy.

Never decoration.

---

# Gesture Rules

Swipe Back

Always enabled.

Pull to Refresh

Available where meaningful.

Pinch

Reserved for future interfaces.

Double Tap

Never required.

---

# Accessibility

Navigation must support

VoiceOver

Switch Control

Keyboard Navigation

Large Text

Reduce Motion

One-handed use

---

# Navigation Checklist

Before approving a screen ask:

Can users reach it in three levels?

Can users return naturally?

Does it follow iOS expectations?

Can VoiceOver navigate it?

Does state restore correctly?

Can users continue interrupted tasks?

If any answer is "No"

The navigation must be redesigned.

---

# Golden Rule

The fastest interface is the one users never think about.

Navigation should disappear.

Only the experience should remain.

